// NFL and AFL player names, first-name + last-name only. 31423 unique names.
// Each entry also includes lastYear (their final/most recent season), used for
// the "guess an older player" scoring bonus.
//
// Two sources, both from the nflverse project (https://github.com/nflverse/nflverse-data,
// licensed CC BY 4.0 -- attribution required):
//  1. Players whose career reaches 1974 or later come from the nflverse player table
//     (unchanged from the previous version of this file; 23985 names).
//     NOTE: the cutoff is "last season >= 1974", not "rookie season >= 1974" as an
//     earlier version of this header said -- e.g. Joe Namath (rookie 1965) is in it.
//  2. Players whose career ended in 1973 or earlier come from nflverse's historical
//     season rosters (1920-1973), which nflverse builds from the NFL's official
//     roster data. This covers the NFL from 1920 and the AFL (1960-1969). lastYear
//     is the last season the player appears on a roster. 7438 names.
//     Not included: players who only appeared in the All-America Football Conference
//     (1946-49) -- 336 people -- and the short-lived 1926/1936-37/1940-41
//     American Football Leagues (not in the source). Generational suffixes (Jr., Sr.)
//     were dropped from these 7438 names because the game reads the last word
//     of a name as the last initial. Early-1920s players whose first name is unknown
//     to the source (listed as "? Surname", 23 of them) were left out.
// Where two players share a name, the entry uses whichever earns the smaller bonus
// (ties: the more recent player), so a modern player's name always outranks an older
// namesake.
//
// Compiled for use in "Daily Duograms" — Copyright © 2026 UVM87 Games.
// Note: the underlying player names are public factual data and are not,
// by themselves, copyrightable -- this notice covers the game code that
// uses this data, not the raw names.
//
// Works in both the browser (just declares a global "athletes" array)
// and in Node (also exports it via module.exports), so the same file
// works whether it is loaded with a <script> tag or require()'d.

